Pattern: Airdrop reflex loop
Participants optimize for point farming or one off rewards instead of durable protocol usage.
Observed in: Consumer crypto apps, incentive launch chains, points to token programs.
Behavior: Activity spikes around reward windows, then falls off hard once emissions or speculation fades.
Risk impl: Growth metrics become misleading because they measure extraction behavior, not adoption.
Frequency: high

Pattern: Yield masking product market fit
Subsidies hide the fact that demand disappears when incentives are removed.
Observed in: DeFi liquidity programs, restaking style incentive layers, liquidity bootstrapping protocols.
Behavior: TVL and volume look strong during emissions, then collapse when the subsidy ends.
Risk impl: The protocol confuses liquidity rental with true economic demand.
Frequency: high

Pattern: Reward inversion
Users who create long term value are paid less than the users who game short term metrics.
Observed in: Points systems, social protocols, marketplace incentive schemes.
Behavior: High frequency, low quality actions get rewarded more cleanly than slower, higher value contributions.
Risk impl: The system trains participants to behave against the protocol’s real objectives.
Frequency: medium

Pattern: Emissions dependency trap
The protocol cannot remove incentives without breaking the activity it created.
Observed in: Token launch chains, liquidity mining protocols, growth stage governance systems.
Behavior: Every attempt to reduce rewards triggers complaints, activity drop-off, or governance resistance.
Risk impl: The protocol becomes locked into subsidizing its own illusion of health.
Frequency: high

Pattern: Quorum math exploitation 
Anti whale caps creating a threshold below which coordinated actors can reliably control outcomes without triggering abuse detection.
Observed in: Token weighted governance systems, community treasury pools.
Behavior: A voting weight cap per wallet is designed to prevent whale dominance. But if the quorum threshold can be reached by a small number of coordinated wallets each sitting just below the cap, the protection becomes nominal. The system flags no individual actor as dominant while the aggregate outcome is controlled.
Risk impl: The governance design appears robust at the single actor level while remaining exploitable at the coordination layer. Sybil resistance and whale resistance are treated as the same problem when they require different mitigations.
Frequency: medium

Pattern: Ceremony trust decay
The social proof around a cryptographic trusted setup thins over time as the novelty of the ceremony fades and contributor legibility disappears.
Observed in: Groth16-based ZK protocols, phase-2 ceremony dependent privacy infrastructure.
Behavior: At launch, ceremony contributors are known and the setup feels credible. Over time, contributors become harder to verify, attestations get buried in documentation, and new circuits ship with quiet ceremonies that receive less scrutiny than the original. Each new ceremony resets the trust assumption without anyone formally acknowledging it.
Risk impl: The protocol's privacy claims rest on a social trust layer that degrades passively. Users and integrators cannot verify the assumption without archaeology. A challenge, even an unfounded one, finds no clean response path.
Frequency: medium

Pattern: Tiered Access Front-Running
Differential signal timing creates extractable value for early recipients at the direct expense of later recipients
Observed in: Trading signal platforms, prediction market alert systems, DeFi analytics protocols
Behavior: Premium participants receive actionable intelligence before public participants. The time delta between tiers creates a structured extraction window where informed participants systematically advantage themselves against uninformed ones
Risk Impl: Trust erosion as public participants recognize the structural disadvantage. Regulatory exposure in jurisdictions treating differential signal distribution as market manipulation.
Frequency: high
Confidence: Established - the mechanism is well documented in traditional finance under front running and selective disclosure frameworks; the Web3 manifestation is newer but structurally identical

Pattern: Stake weighted plutocracy
Active voting bodies are selected purely by stake size. Governance outcomes therefore reflect capital concentration rather than informed judgment. A sufficiently capitalized actor can dominate the active set without ever producing a provably wrong output making slashing ineffective as a deterrent.
Observed: DAO governance systems, liquid staking token governance, oracle committee selection, validator voting systems, treasury allocation voting, bug bounty judgment networks
Behavior: Voting power tracks capital holdings. The largest holders define validity, set parameters, and control escalation paths. Coordinated behavior among top holders is rational and undetectable when each individual action remains within policy bounds. The governance surface appears decentralized while control is structurally concentrated.
Risk Impl: Consensus becomes capital selection rather than judgment aggregation. Denial, censorship, and selective confirmation become available attack vectors. Slashing catches provably wrong individual judgments but misses coordinated threshold behavior.
Confidence: Canonical — extensively documented across DAO governance research, LST governance incidents, and oracle manipulation case studies 

Pattern: Reputation Without Behavioral Anchor
Reputation systems accumulate scores based on activity metrics (execution count, finding volume, transaction frequency) rather than alignment with protocol objectives. 
Observed: Bug bounty reputation systems, agent identity registries, validator track records, DeFi protocol contributor scores
Behavior: High reputation actors gain privileged access, reduced scrutiny, or governance weight. Behavioral drift occurs without triggering reputation decay because the metrics being tracked are orthogonal to the behaviors that matter. Trust scores continue rising despite strategic misalignment.
Risk Impl: Actors causing disproportionate damage precisely because their reputation suppresses the scrutiny that would otherwise detect drift.
Confidence: Emerging — two confirmed protocol observations; the mechanism is theoretically well established but on chain manifestations are still early

